IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `mlr-core`

-- THE KEEL'S DEFINITIONS: the Money Laundering, Terrorist Financing and Transfer of Funds
-- (Information on the Payer) Regulations 2017 (S.I. 2017/692), United Kingdom, Part 1:
-- regulations 3 (general interpretation), 4 (business relationship), 5 and 6 (beneficial
-- owner) and 7 (supervisory authorities), and regulation 2 (prescribed regulations).  Regulation 1
-- (citation; the commencement date is a constant in mlr-core.l4) and the enacting words are
-- machinery and are inert (see NOTES.md).
--
-- The text is a plain-text rendering made by a converter script; it is not the official text,
-- and nothing here is verified against the official text.  A `-- src:N | text` line is a
-- verbatim slice of the rendering (line N of uksi-2017-692.txt) and `@ref ... (src:N)` ties
-- the rule under it to those lines; srcrefs.py checks both.
--
-- HOW TO READ AN ANSWER.  A rule answers JUST TRUE or JUST FALSE only where the text itself
-- says so in its own words (a definition that says "means", a condition introduced by "if",
-- "only if", "unless" ..., a list closed by "only", a flat negative).  Everywhere else (a list
-- that "includes", an undefined term, a point outside the text, a grant limited by its own
-- wording) it answers NOTHING.  NOTHING is never FALSE.  Each branch says which, in a comment.
-- A borrowed term (an Act or instrument not among the sources) is asked as a plain
-- BOOLEAN fact, and the comment names where it is defined.
--
-- NAMES.  Every top-level name begins with the label of the regulation it implements.  A
-- term of reg 3(1) that merely points elsewhere in these Regulations is a STRING naming the
-- regulation it points at; the definition itself is the row of the part that owns that
-- regulation (PLAN.md).

§ `reg 2 Prescribed regulations`

-- The provision gives these Regulations a status under another Act (FSMA, not among the sources).
-- It is a pointer: what the Regulations are prescribed for, in the text's words.
-- src:11 | These Regulations are prescribed for the purposes of sections 168(4)(b) (appointment of persons to carry out investigations in particular cases) and 402(1)(b) (power of the FCA to institute proceedings for certain other offences) of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 .
@ref reg 2 (src:11)
GIVETH A STRING
`reg 2 these Regulations are prescribed for the purposes of` MEANS "sections 168(4)(b) (appointment of persons to carry out investigations in particular cases) and 402(1)(b) (power of the FCA to institute proceedings for certain other offences) of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000"

